  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and wetter \nconditions is expected through Sunday.\n\nThis afternoon, a cold front is located south of the area over \nsouthern North Carolina. Meanwhile, high pressure (~1030 mb) is \ncentered over the eastern Great Lakes and ridging south into the Mid \nAtlantic. Widespread cloud cover and much cooler conditions over a \nmajority of the area this afternoon as wedge airmass develops over \nthe local area. Seeing two areas of showers on radar currently, \nlight to moderate rain over roughly the northern half of the area \nand area of steadier rain just to the south of the forecast area \nover North Carolina. Tonight, the front will remain stalled well \nsouth of the local area with waves of low pressure likely moving \nalong the front. In addition, another front will begin to approach \nfrom the NW later tonight into Sunday. Tropical moisture streaming \nup between the fronts (PWATs 1.6-1.8\", 2 sigma above normal) will \nbring additional rounds of rain tonight through Sunday.\n\nOvercast, cool, and showery conditions continue through the day on \nSunday. The heaviest rain will likely be across far southern and SE \nportions of the area (closer to the front). ~1.00\"+ QPF is possible \ncloser to the Albemarle Sound and portions of northeast North \nCarolina. Lighter rain/drizzle is expected for much of the remainder \nof the area with the CAD airmass in place. Instability will be \nminimal, with any thunder chances restricted to the far SE. \nPersistent clouds and rain will cap Sunday highs in the mid 60s to \nlower 70s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry next week.\n\nAny rain comes to an end from NW to SE late Sunday night into early \nMonday morning as a second, stronger cold front crosses the area. \nClearing skies and a brief downslope flow will allow for high \ntemperatures to climb into the lower to middle 70s on Monday. A \nstronger surge of cold air advection arrives Monday night as ~1024 \nmb high pressure builds over the Ohio Valley. Overnight lows fall \ninto the upper 40s NW to mid 50s SE. A high confidence, autumn \npattern takes hold Tuesday through late next week as strong high \npressure anchors over the Eastern US and subtle troughing sets up \naloft. Highs will start out in the mid 60s to lower 70s on Tuesday, \ngradually warming each day. Efficient radiational cooling is possible \neach night with lows dropping back into the 40s and 50s. The next \nchances for rain are likely not until next weekend (at the \nearliest).",
